# Data Access Design Rules

Applies whenever a user story touches persistence — reads, writes, reporting, or schema.
Supported providers: **Oracle**, **SQL Server**, **PostgreSQL**.

You are a design agent. These rules tell you how to decide and document a data-access approach.
You must not implement it.

**You have no live database access.** There is no database tool available to you. You cannot connect,
query, or introspect. Everything you know about the schema must come from a file in the repository or
from the user. Never read credentials from `appsettings*.json` and never attempt a connection.

---

## 1. Declare the provider first

Determine the provider from the repository, in this order:

1. EF Core provider package in the `.csproj`:
   - `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer` → SQL Server
   - `Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL` → PostgreSQL
   - `Oracle.EntityFrameworkCore` → Oracle
2. The `UseSqlServer` / `UseNpgsql` / `UseOracle` call in DI registration.
3. Connection-string shape in `appsettings*.json` — read it **only** to identify provider, server and
   database name. Never read, echo, or copy a password, key, or secret. Never put a connection string
   in the design document.

Record the provider and, if stated anywhere in the repo, the server version. Several rules below
change with the version.

If the codebase targets **more than one** provider, say so and apply §3.

---

## 2. Resolving the schema

Take the first source that answers the question and stop:

> You have no direct database connection. Steps (a) and (b) are file reads. Step (c) is a script
> you may run if a terminal tool is available. Step (d) is asking the user.

**a. DbContext and migrations.** `DbSet<T>` properties, `OnModelCreating`,
`IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` classes, and the `Migrations/` folder. This is authoritative for
everything the application already maps.

**b. OKF schema bundle committed to the repository.** Look under `docs/db/<provider>/` for an
Open Knowledge Format bundle — a directory of markdown concept files, one per table or view, each
with YAML frontmatter (`type`, `title`, `description`, `tags`, `timestamp`) and a body containing
columns, indexes and keys.

The layout is `docs/db/<provider>/<schema>/{tables,views,routines}/<object>.md`. Objects are
scoped by schema, so `sales.orders` and `staging.orders` are distinct concepts — always confirm
which schema a story means before designing against it.

Navigate it; do not read it whole. The same reading discipline applies as to source code:

1. Start at `docs/db/<provider>/index.md` to see which schemas exist.
2. Follow to `<schema>/index.md`, then `tables/index.md`, `views/index.md` or `routines/index.md`.
   These are listings, not reading queues.
3. Open only the concept files for objects the story actually touches. A 200-object bundle should
   normally cost you three to six file reads.
4. Foreign keys are markdown links to the referenced concept. Follow a link only when the story
   needs that object.

**Check `routines/` before proposing any new function or stored procedure.** A routine that already
does the job — or most of it — changes the design: call or extend it rather than adding a duplicate.
If an existing routine is close but not exact, say so explicitly and recommend extending it, or
justify why a new one is needed. Proposing a new routine without having looked at `routines/` is an
error.

Check the `timestamp` in the frontmatter. If it predates the newest migration in `Migrations/`, the
bundle may be stale — say so under Open Questions and treat its row counts as unvalidated.

This is the only source for objects that exist in the database but are not in the model.

**c. Refresh the snapshot yourself — only if you have a terminal tool.** If the snapshot is missing
or stale and you can run commands, run the repository script:

```
python scripts/dump_schema.py --list                                    # confirm what it detects
python scripts/dump_schema.py --objects <comma separated object names>  # then dump
```

The script discovers the connection string in `appsettings*.json`, detects whether the application
runs on PostgreSQL, SQL Server or Oracle, and writes an OKF bundle to `docs/db/<provider>/`. It needs no
per-application configuration. If several connection strings exist it will refuse to guess — re-run
with `--name <ConnectionName>`.

Rules for this step:
- Run **only** this script. Never write ad-hoc SQL, never invoke `sqlcmd` / `psql` / `sqlplus`
  directly, and never place a credential on a command line.
- **Do not open `appsettings*.json` yourself to obtain credentials.** The script parses it; you do
  not. Anything you read enters the transcript and may be copied into the design document. You may
  read a connection string only to note the provider, host and database name — never a password —
  and none of it goes in the document.
- If a password is a `${VAR}` placeholder or absent, the script says so. Tell the user which
  environment variable to set. Do not ask them to tell you the value.
- If the script exits reporting a missing driver, tell the user the exact `pip install` line it
  printed.
- If the script is absent, or no terminal tool is available to you, go to (d).

**d. Ask the user.** If the story references objects you cannot resolve from (a) or (b), **stop and
ask before writing the design**. Do not guess table names, column names, types, nullability,
relationships, or row counts. A design built on invented schema is worse than no design, because it
looks authoritative and the coding agent will act on it.

Ask precisely. Name what is missing and what you need:

> I can't resolve `RPT_TRADE_SUMMARY` from the DbContext, the migrations, or a schema snapshot in the
> repo. To design this correctly I need, for each object the story touches:
> - DDL or a column list (name, type, nullable)
> - primary key and foreign keys
> - existing indexes
> - approximate row count
> - whether it is a table or a view, and which team owns it
>
> Either paste it, or commit a schema dump to `/docs/db/` and tell me the path.

If the user provides schema in chat, restate it in the design document so the coding agent has it too.

**Never ask for connection details.** Do not ask for a hostname, port, service name, username,
password, or connection string. You have no tool that can use them, and they must not appear in the
transcript or in the design document. What you need is the schema itself, not access to it. If the
user offers credentials, decline and repeat the schema request.

**If you cannot ask** — you are running unattended, with no user to respond — do not guess and do not
proceed. Write the design document with `Status: BLOCKED` and fill in only the sections you could
complete honestly, listing the exact missing schema under Open Questions.

### Objects present in the database but not in the model

When the story needs an object that exists in the database but not in the DbContext, the design must
state which of these applies and why:

- Add a new entity plus configuration (a table that will be written to).
- Map as a **keyless entity** (`HasNoKey`) — read-only views and reporting projections.
- Do not map at all; access through raw SQL projected into a DTO.
- A migration is required (the object does not exist yet, or must change).

Also state who owns the object. If another team owns it, do not propose schema changes to it — raise
it as an Open Question.

